<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Shule</h1><p class="st-meta">200 BCE – 790 CE (approx.) · capital Kashgar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The oasis kingdom of Kashgar at the western end of the Tarim Basin, a crossroads where the Silk Roads met before climbing the Pamirs.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Shule, the Chinese name for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a>, was a city-state at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamir-mountains">Pamirs</a>, recorded by Han sources from the 2nd century BCE. Its people spoke an Eastern Iranian language related to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saka">Saka</a>, and it controlled the routes west to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-ferghana">Ferghana</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Bactria</a> and south towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Shule was ruled by local kings who switched allegiance between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, Kushan, Han and later Turkic powers. In the 70s CE the Han general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ban-chao">Ban Chao</a> used it as his base for reconquering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-regions">Western Regions</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> was strong, and the city's markets traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, horses and jade. Under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a> its kings of the Pei family ruled as one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-garrisons-of-anxi">Four Garrisons of Anxi</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tibetan-empire">Tibetan Empire</a> took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim</a> garrisons around 790, ending <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> authority. In the 10th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a> became the capital of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kara-khanid-khanate">Kara-Khanid Khanate</a>, whose rulers converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and turned the city into a centre of Turkic Muslim culture.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-shule.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Shule" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Schreiber · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tarimbecken_3._Jahrhundert.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>200 BCE – 790 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kashgar</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Saka (Eastern Iranian)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Shule, Kashgar</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=295&amp;focus=kingdom-of-shule" aria-label="Kingdom of Shule on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6557 764 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Shule lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6877 1020L7771 1192"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7771 1192h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6877 1020h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6877 1020h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kashgar. <a href="/atlas?year=295&amp;focus=kingdom-of-shule">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

